One common source of vulnerabilities in Java web applications is the improper handling of user input when performing file operations, which can lead to **Path Traversal** attacks. This allows an attacker to access files outside of the intended directory (like `/etc/passwd`).

```java
import java.io.IOException;
import java.nio.file.Files;
import java.nio.file.Path;
import java.nio.file.Paths;

public class FileHandler {
    // This method is vulnerable to Path Traversal attacks if 'filename' contains "../"
    public byte[] readFile(String filename) throws IOException {
        Path filePath = Paths.get("uploads", filename);
        return Files.readAllBytes(filePath); // Returns file content regardless of location
    }
}
```
***
```java
import java.io.IOException;
import java.nio.file.Files;
import java.nio.file.Path;
import java.nio.file.Paths;

public class FileHandler {
    // This method is secure because it validates the input against a base directory
    public byte[] readFile(String filename) throws IOException {
        Path baseDirectory = Paths.get("uploads").toAbsolutePath().normalize();
        // Resolve the path and normalize to remove any "../ tricks
        Path targetPath = baseDirectory.resolve(filename).normalize();

        // Check if the resulting path is still within the base directory
        if (!targetPath.startsWith(baseDirectory)) {
            throw new SecurityException("Access Denied: Invalid file path.");
        }

        return Files.readAllBytes(targetPath);
    }
}
```

